Tests for checking the behavior of subshell and proof_subshell.

Includes material from:
   #2242 (scoping regression in proof_subshell)
   #2243 (bindings escape proof_subshell)
   #2306 (bizarre environment capturing in subshell / proof_subshell)
   #2751 (exact scoping semantics of interactive subshells)

as well as further tests developed in the course of #3529.

Up until #3259 in November 2026, the expectation, and eventually the
actual behavior, was that a subshell would run as a nested scope in
the lexical context where the "subshell" (or "proof_subshell") command
was monad-bound. Thus for example in things like
   do { let s = subshell(); let x = 6; s; return 0; }
the subshell would see the value of x.

In #3259, in pursuit of the mutually entangled goals of disentangling
the SAWScript interpreter from the typechecker, and of being able to
typecheck a whole file at once, restrictions were applied.

These forms are supposed to be accepted:

   do { ... subshell (); ... };
   do { ... proof_subshell (); ... };
   somefunction ... (subshell ()) ...
   somefunction ... (proof_subshell ()) ...

as well as the same forms as in do-blocks at the syntactic top level.
Other usages should be rejected. That includes applying the unit
argument and monad-binding the result separately, passing around the
value without the unit argument applied, and also trying to place
a subshell as the last value argument of a do-block.

This is to enable capturing a typing environment to typecheck the
interactive subshell in the correct context, even if we typecheck the
whole file before executing any of it and therefore execute the
subshell much later. There are assorted complications with trying to
capture a typing environment at the monad-bind point rather than at
the point where the builtin is referenced, and since there's not
really any good reason to allow those to be different points, we don't
any more. See the discussion at the top of Subshells.hs for further
information.

The code in #3259 also intentionally changes the behavior of the
latter two forms if the function being called is a SAWScript function
(rather than a builtin, which is the common case with proof_subshell):
the subshell runs in the context of the caller, regardless of where
the argument value is monad-bound in the callee. This is to make the
common case of proof_subshell work and is not intended to be
particularly principled.
